SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"México en el Debate General de la 66ª Conferencia General del OIEA","publisher":"International Atomic Energy Agency","publication_date":"2022-09-26","url":"https://www.iaea.org/sites/default/files/22/09/mexico-gc66.pdf","claim":"Mexico publicly supported nuclear nonproliferation, IAEA safeguards, nuclear security, and nuclear/radiological safety.","locator":"PDF pp. 1–2, paragraphs beginning “México reconoce la labor del OIEA” and “Salvaguardias del OIEA”","provisional_id":"WEB-1B-001","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.iaea.org/sites/default/files/22/09/mexico-gc66.pdf|pp.1-2|support for safeguards and nuclear safety","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["1B"],"methodology_ids":["1B.1","1B.3","1B.4"],"source_type":"official_international_statement","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official Mexican delegation statement to the IAEA General Conference.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Issued during 2022 and directly describes the government's stated nuclear posture.","ruler_attribution":"The statement reflects the federal executive's diplomatic policy during López Obrador's presidency, though it does not establish his personal authorship of each technical measure.","contrary_evidence":["The statement is official self-description and does not independently verify implementation quality."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.3","1B.4"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"México en el Debate General de la 66ª Conferencia General del OIEA","publisher":"International Atomic Energy Agency","publication_date":"2022-09-26","url":"https://www.iaea.org/sites/default/files/22/09/mexico-gc66.pdf","claim":"Mexico endorsed effective and transparent peer review and described nuclear safety and physical security as continuing priorities.","locator":"PDF p. 2, paragraphs on the Seventh Meeting of Contracting Parties and nuclear/radiological/physical security","provisional_id":"WEB-1B-002","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.iaea.org/sites/default/files/22/09/mexico-gc66.pdf|p.2|peer review and nuclear security","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["1B"],"methodology_ids":["1B.3","1B.7","1B.9"],"source_type":"official_international_statement","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary diplomatic statement with identifiable page locators.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"2022 statement; the cited institutional designation covered 2022–2026.","ruler_attribution":"Supports attribution to national foreign-policy and regulatory posture, not proof of operational command-and-control performance.","contrary_evidence":["The statement supplies commitments and institutional participation but little adverse testing of safeguards."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.7","1B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Country List: Mexico","publisher":"International Atomic Energy Agency Office of Legal Affairs","publication_date":"2026-02-24","url":"https://ola.iaea.org/Applications/FactSheets/Country/Detail?code=MX","claim":"Mexico has long-standing NPT/Latin American nuclear safeguards arrangements, an Additional Protocol, and participation in nuclear-accident notification and assistance conventions.","locator":"Safeguards Agreements section, lines 112–128; Nuclear Accident Notification and Assistance entries, lines 43–51","provisional_id":"WEB-1B-003","canonical_fact_key":"https://ola.iaea.org/Applications/FactSheets/Country/Detail?code=MX|safeguards and accident conventions|Mexico status","disposition":"context","chapter_ids":["1B"],"methodology_ids":["1B.1","1B.4","1B.6"],"source_type":"official_treaty_registry","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"IAEA treaty and safeguards registry; historical entry dates are explicit.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"The arrangements predate 2022 and therefore establish inherited baseline rather than a new 2022 action.","ruler_attribution":"Provides inherited national posture; López Obrador inherited these commitments and did not create them in 2022.","contrary_evidence":["Registry status alone does not demonstrate the quality of 2022 implementation or inspection cooperation."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.4","1B.6"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"México: La militarización de la seguridad pública amenaza los derechos humanos","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-08-26","url":"https://www.hrw.org/es/news/2022/08/26/mexico-la-militarizacion-de-la-seguridad-publica-amenaza-los-derechos-humanos","claim":"López Obrador's government proposed transferring formal control of the National Guard to the Defense Ministry and extending military public-security authority indefinitely.","locator":"Paragraphs 430–432","provisional_id":"WEB-1B-004","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.hrw.org/es/news/2022/08/26/mexico-la-militarizacion-de-la-seguridad-publica-amenaza-los-derechos-humanos|430-432|National Guard transfer proposal","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["1B"],"methodology_ids":["1B.3","1B.5","1B.7","1B.10"],"source_type":"human_rights_monitoring_report","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous HRW reporting with direct attribution to the president's announced policy.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"August 2022.","ruler_attribution":"The proposal was publicly announced by the president and his secretary of the interior, establishing strong executive responsibility for the policy direction.","contrary_evidence":["Congress, courts, and constitutional constraints limited implementation; the proposal was defended as necessary for security."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.5","1B.7","1B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"México: Militarizar la seguridad pública generará más violaciones de derechos humanos y perpetuará la impunidad","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022-09-09","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/es/latest/news/2022/09/mexico-militarizing-public-security-human-rights-violations-impunity/","claim":"Amnesty reported that López Obrador's proposed reforms placed the National Guard under military control and warned that militarization could worsen abuses and impunity.","locator":"Paragraphs 63–82","provisional_id":"WEB-1B-005","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.amnesty.org/es/latest/news/2022/09/mexico-militarizing-public-security-human-rights-violations-impunity/|63-82|militarization and impunity warning","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["1B"],"methodology_ids":["1B.3","1B.5","1B.7","1B.10"],"source_type":"human_rights_ngo_statement","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous NGO assessment quoting the legislative proposal and relevant UN concerns.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"September 2022.","ruler_attribution":"The proposal was initiated by the president and his administration, though Senate approval was a separate institutional act.","contrary_evidence":["The government and congressional supporters argued that the reform preserved the Guard's civilian legal status and improved security capacity."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.5","1B.7","1B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Amnesty International Report 2022/23: Mexico","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2023-02-28","url":"https://www.ecoi.net/en/document/2089563.html","claim":"By the end of 2022, militarization had expanded, the National Guard and Defense Ministry received large numbers of human-rights complaints, and transparency and accountability remained weak.","locator":"Summary and background paragraphs 34–43","provisional_id":"WEB-1B-006","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.ecoi.net/en/document/2089563.html|34-43|militarization complaints and accountability","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["1B"],"methodology_ids":["1B.3","1B.5","1B.7","1B.10"],"source_type":"annual_human_rights_report","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Annual country report with specific 2022 figures and institutional findings.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective report covering 2022.","ruler_attribution":"The report concerns federal policies promoted during López Obrador's administration, while recognizing state-level and institutional variation.","contrary_evidence":["The Supreme Court upheld some military participation and reviewed other measures, showing institutional contestation rather than unconstrained executive control."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.5","1B.7","1B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 Country Report on Human Rights Practices: Mexico","publisher":"U.S. Department of State","publication_date":"2023-03-20","url":"https://www.ecoi.net/en/document/2089118.html","claim":"The 2022 record included credible reports of unlawful killings, forced disappearances, torture, media restrictions, and very low prosecution rates involving security forces and other officials.","locator":"Executive summary, paragraphs 40–41","provisional_id":"WEB-1B-007","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.ecoi.net/en/document/2089118.html|40-41|2022 security abuses and impunity","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["1B"],"methodology_ids":["1B.5","1B.7","1B.10"],"source_type":"government_human_rights_report","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Country report drawing on public, governmental, and independent sources; claims are explicitly qualified as credible reports.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Covers calendar year 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Relevant to federal security policy and oversight, but not proof that López Obrador personally ordered individual abuses.","contrary_evidence":["The report also records legal safeguards and institutional variation; attribution is strongest for policy architecture and weaker for individual incidents."],"lenses":["1B.5","1B.7","1B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"La Estrategia Nacional de Seguridad funciona y tiene una directriz clara","publisher":"Secretaría de Seguridad y Protección Ciudadana","publication_date":"2022-12-02","url":"https://www.gob.mx/sspc/prensa/la-estrategia-nacional-de-seguridad-funciona-y-tiene-una-directriz-clara-sspc","claim":"The government described its national security strategy as combining prevention, coordination, police training, human-rights respect, and armed-forces participation, while reporting reductions in several crimes.","locator":"Paragraphs 51–90","provisional_id":"WEB-1B-008","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.gob.mx/sspc/prensa/la-estrategia-nacional-de-seguridad-funciona-y-tiene-una-directriz-clara-sspc|51-90|official security strategy claims","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["1B"],"methodology_ids":["1B.1","1B.5","1B.8","1B.10"],"source_type":"official_government_communication","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Primary official account with precise date and reported metrics, but self-reported and not independent evaluation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"December 2022.","ruler_attribution":"The meeting and strategy were presented under López Obrador's presidency and concerned federal executive policy.","contrary_evidence":["HRW, Amnesty, the U.S. State Department, and UN mechanisms reported abuses, weak accountability, and risks from militarization."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.5","1B.8","1B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Decreto por el que se aprueba el Programa Nacional de Seguridad Pública 2022–2024","publisher":"Diario Oficial de la Federación","publication_date":"2022-12-01","url":"https://sidof.segob.gob.mx/notas/docFuente/5673251","claim":"López Obrador formally approved a binding federal National Public Security Program for 2022–2024, with implementation monitoring assigned to federal agencies.","locator":"Articles First, Second, and implementation provisions; signature block","provisional_id":"WEB-1B-009","canonical_fact_key":"https://sidof.segob.gob.mx/notas/docFuente/5673251|arts.1-2 and implementation|2022-2024 security program","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["1B"],"methodology_ids":["1B.3","1B.7","1B.8","1B.10"],"source_type":"official_legal_record","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official gazette decree with legal effect and executive signature.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Issued 1 December 2022 and applicable to 2022–2024.","ruler_attribution":"Direct executive approval establishes formal responsibility for the program's institutional design, not all downstream results.","contrary_evidence":["The decree delegates implementation across agencies and therefore involves shared administrative responsibility."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.7","1B.8","1B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Suprema Corte determina substanciar y resolver de manera prioritaria asuntos relacionados con la Guardia Nacional","publisher":"Suprema Corte de Justicia de la Nación","publication_date":"2022-12-09","url":"https://www.internet2.scjn.gob.mx/red2/comunicados/noticia.asp?id=7176","claim":"The Supreme Court prioritized constitutional cases concerning the National Guard, military criminal rules, and armed-forces participation in public security.","locator":"Press release No. 451/2022, opening paragraph and list of challenged measures","provisional_id":"WEB-1B-010","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.internet2.scjn.gob.mx/red2/comunicados/noticia.asp?id=7176|No.451/2022|priority constitutional review","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["1B"],"methodology_ids":["1B.3","1B.7","1B.8","1B.10"],"source_type":"official_judicial_record","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary Supreme Court communication documenting active constitutional review.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"December 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Demonstrates a material institutional constraint on executive security policy rather than personal conduct by the ruler.","contrary_evidence":["The existence of review does not itself establish that the challenged policy was unlawful or unsafe."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.7","1B.8","1B.10"]}

### Research accounting

- Documents discovered: approximately 35 plausible documents across English- and Spanish-language searches.
- Underlying pages/reports opened or attempted: 24; at least 12 promising source pages or PDFs were inspected successfully or through indexed document text.
- Accepted new evidence units: 10.
- Reused prior evidence: 4 records, principally identity/authority, electricity-reform constraints, National Guard transfer, Senate action, and Supreme Court review.
- Rejected or context-only: 8 candidates, mainly stale treaty baselines, official self-descriptions without independent verification, and post-period material.
- Access-blocked: 9 pages/PDFs, including several gob.mx, OHCHR document-store, CNSNS, Senate, and SCJN pages. Search-result text was not treated as a substitute for independently opened evidence except where the source had already been accepted in the prior ledger.
- Distinct organizations: IAEA, IAEA Office of Legal Affairs, Human Rights Watch, Amnesty International, U.S. Department of State, SSPC, Diario Oficial de la Federación, and Supreme Court of Justice.
- Source types: official international statement, treaty registry, NGO monitoring, government human-rights report, official executive communication, legal gazette, and judicial record.

Lens coverage:

- 1B.1: partial — nonproliferation and official de-escalatory security framing; no direct nuclear-risk decision.
- 1B.2: residual gap — no reliable evidence of nuclear rhetoric or reckless nuclear threats.
- 1B.3: partial — nuclear-safety institutions plus strong evidence on military command-and-control; no direct nuclear command audit.
- 1B.4: partial — safeguards and treaty baseline; no 2022 arms-control or disarmament initiative.
- 1B.5: partial — substantial evidence concerning military public-security deployment and repression risk; no nuclear shielding issue.
- 1B.6: residual gap — no evidence found of ally/proxy/domestic proliferation.
- 1B.7: partial — formal institutions and peer-review commitments contrasted with concerns about militarized oversight and transparency.
- 1B.8: partial — official coordination and judicial channels documented; no nuclear crisis during the period.
- 1B.9: weak/partial — nuclear and radiological safety institutions documented; targeted cyber, biological, and AI searches produced mostly inaccessible or post-period material.
- 1B.10: partial — inherited nonproliferation posture appears stable, while broader security governance became more militarized and accountability risks increased.

I could not update `research-ledger-manifest.json`: the attempt directory was read-only in this execution environment. The ten `WEB-1B-*` records above, together with the seven supplied prior `AMLO22-R*` records, constitute the cumulative manifest content for this turn.

Another chapter-specific search wave would have limited material value unless access is restored for CNSNS, OHCHR, Senate, and cybersecurity/protection-civil records. The remaining gaps are primarily absence-of-evidence questions—nuclear rhetoric, proliferation by proxies, and catastrophic-risk domains—not likely to be resolved by repeating the same web searches.
